"A document schema diagram shows nested document shape, arrays, optional fields, and references between documents.\n\nUse when: Choose embedding versus references for a document database. Explain the accepted structure of a JSON payload.\n\nAvoid when: The principal issue is relational join enforcement or message timing.\n\nVisual grammar: Nested compartments encode embedded objects and arrays. Field annotations state type, required status, and relevant bounds. Reference connectors link independent document identities without implying enforced foreign keys.\n\nBuild: Choose a representative document type and access pattern. Lay out nested objects and bounded array examples. Separate embedded structure from external references. Use indentation or nested cards with generous padding and keep reference arrows outside field lists.\n\nCheck: Required fields and nullable values are distinguished. Arrays show item shape rather than an invented fixed count. Schema rules and database enforcement are labeled accurately. Nested borders do not cross property names or reference labels.\n\nPitfalls: Embedding an unbounded history without showing its growth implications. Assuming a reference receives relational referential-integrity guarantees.\n\nExample: Order embeds its delivery address and line snapshots but references Customer by identity.\n\nAlternatives: Relational schema diagram, Schema compatibility graph."
